"Stacks" and "queues" are explicit syllabus items. Official DA papers have asked students to:

- match access disciplines (LIFO, FIFO, lookup) with stacks, queues and hash tables;
- trace a sequence of double-ended-queue operations and report a value (NAT);
- trace pseudocode that pushes and pops a stack inside a loop (NAT).

Other natural question forms are postfix evaluation, which output sequences a stack can produce, circular-queue index arithmetic, and the cost of queue operations built from stacks or Python lists. Recursion and depth-first search run on stacks, and breadth-first search runs on a queue, so these ideas reappear in recursion and graph questions.

