definition:
A **rooted tree** is a connected acyclic graph with one vertex chosen as the **root**; every other node has exactly one **parent**. A **binary tree** is either empty or a root together with a **left** and a **right** subtree, each of which is again a binary tree. Every node therefore has at most two children, and left and right are distinguished.

**Terminology** (the convention used here):

- a **leaf** has no children; an **internal node** has at least one;
- the **depth** (level) of a node is the number of edges from the root, so the root is at level 0;
- the **height** $h$ is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path: a single node has $h = 0$ and the empty tree $h = -1$. Some books count nodes instead, which adds 1;
- a **full** (proper) tree has 0 or 2 children at every node; a **complete** tree has every level full except possibly the last, which is filled from the left; a **perfect** tree has every internal node with 2 children and all leaves on the same level.

**Traversals** visit every node once, in $\Theta(n)$ time:

- **preorder**: node, left subtree, right subtree;
- **inorder**: left subtree, node, right subtree;
- **postorder**: left subtree, right subtree, node;
- **level order**: level by level, left to right, using a queue (breadth-first).

