definition:
For functions $f$ and $g$ from the positive integers to the non-negative reals:

- $f(n) = O(g(n))$ if there are constants $c > 0$ and $n_0$ with $f(n) \le c\,g(n)$ for all $n \ge n_0$ (an **upper bound**);
- $f(n) = \Omega(g(n))$ if there are constants $c > 0$ and $n_0$ with $f(n) \ge c\,g(n)$ for all $n \ge n_0$ (a **lower bound**);
- $f(n) = \Theta(g(n))$ if both hold (a **tight bound**);
- $f(n) = o(g(n))$ if $f(n)/g(n) \to 0$, and $f(n) = \omega(g(n))$ if $f(n)/g(n) \to \infty$ (strictly slower or faster growth).

"Worst case", "best case" and "average case" are three different functions of $n$, and each can be described with any of these notations. "The worst case is $\Theta(n^2)$" says more than "the algorithm is $O(n^2)$".

